## 5. Using Your Brain (`brain/`)

Your brain is your persistent memory (`node harness/engine/brain.mjs`, `brain` below). It holds
cards (people, projects, tools, skills, references, notes, contexts) joined by edges, each edge
with a *why*. Forty verbs; `brain <verb> --help` prints the exact shape of any of them.

**Run `brain <verb> --help` before a verb you have not used this session. Never guess flags.**

### Walk the graph first. Search is the last resort.
The brain is a graph, and you read it by walking it, not by guessing words at it. When you need
something, in this order:

1. `brain context <slug>`: the roster of the loaded desk. Most of what a desk needs is on it.
2. `brain relations <slug>`: the edges out of a card the roster gave you, each with its why.
   Follow them. A product card leads to its research, its ideas, its structure; a research card
   leads to the file. Two hops from the roster reaches nearly everything a desk owns.
3. `brain get <slug> --body` for the card itself once the graph has named it.
4. `brain search` only when the roster and the edges have not named the thing, and then with
   keywords, not a sentence: `brain search "pocket bible"`, never `brain search "what did NHB say
   about bible offers"`. Search is full-text over one-line descriptions. It finds words, not
   meaning.

If you reached for search first, you skipped the map. If search found something the graph did
not, the graph is missing an edge: add it before you move on.

### Every card is wired, the moment it is made
A card with no edges is invisible to every desk: the roster never prints it and only search can
reach it, which is the failure above. Minting a card and linking it are one act, in the same
command block, never "later":

- **Where it belongs:** `part_of` the project, product or thing it is about.
- **Who needs it:** `in_context` every desk whose work it serves, one edge per desk, each with a why
  that says what that desk takes from it.
- **What it sits beside:** `about <card>` when an agent should read it with another card. There is
  no `see_also` or `related_to`; use `about`.

This holds for notes and references as much as for skills and tools. A research file on disk
with no card, or a card with one edge, is a note nobody will find. The test: after minting, run
`brain context <desk>` for each desk you linked and see the card on the roster.

The verbs are `part_of` / `has_part`, `in_context`, `serves` / `served_by`, `depends_on` /
`do_before`, `contains` / `advances`, and the weak `about` and `mentions`. Any other verb you
type registers itself as a new verb (the engine prints "registered new verb"); that is how a
typo becomes a permanent verb. If you see that line and did not mean it, `brain unlink` and
`brain register --unregister-verb <name>`.

The one rule that trips agents: verbs that act on a card take the slug **positionally**
(`get`, `peek`, `relations`, `context`, `set`, `link`, `body`). Verbs that list what is
attached to a card take **`--for <slug>`** (`tools`, `skills`, `files`). `--for` refuses a
context on purpose: a context's equipment is read with `brain context <slug>`.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| What contexts exist, which are loaded | `brain contexts` |
| Load a desk (prints its members) | `brain context <slug>` · unload: `brain context --drop <slug>` |
| One card, full | `brain get <slug> --body` · one line: `brain peek <slug>` |
| A card's edges, both directions, with whys | `brain relations <slug>` |
| Skills or tools that serve a thing (not a context) | `brain skills --for <slug>` · `brain tools --for <slug>` |
| Find by text | `brain search "<terms>"` (`--entity skill` to see shaded equipment) |
| Cohort by filter | `brain list --entity tool --edge in_context:<context>` |
| New card | `brain new --entity <entity> --slug <slug> --description "<one line>"` |
| Set a field | `brain set <slug> --field profile.command --to "<value>"` |
| Wire a card into a context | `brain link <slug> --verb in_context --to <context> --why "<why>"` |
| A skill or tool serves a thing | `brain link <slug> --verb serves --to <thing> --why "<why>"` |
| Write a card's body | `echo "<prose>" | brain body <slug>` |
| Working memory (never by hand) | `node harness/components/brain/tools/wm.mjs show` · see 5a |

Entities you will use: `context` (profile.name, profile.mission), `skill` (profile.name, cadence,
last_verified), `tool` (profile.name, command, path, url), `project`, `reference`, `note`.
Put a tool's command in `profile.command` and its path in `profile.path`, not in the description;
the description is the one line the roster prints, so write it as the line that lets an agent
choose.

### A context is equipped, not just minted
A context with only a working memory card is an empty desk: loading it shows one line, and every
skill and tool stays shaded in search. When you mint a context, wire what serves that world in the
same turn:

```bash
# a skill card per seat skill that applies (one card, wired into every context it serves)
brain new --entity skill --slug skill-opus-writer --description "Writes market-facing copy on Opus 4.5 with a voice pack"
brain link skill-opus-writer --verb in_context --to wolf-marketing --why "all WOBS copy runs on it"
# a tool card per script or service the world uses
brain new --entity tool --slug deploy-wobs --description "rsync the static site to dev then prod"
brain set deploy-wobs --field profile.command --to "deploy/push.sh dev|prod"
brain link deploy-wobs --verb in_context --to wobs-sites --why "the only way the site ships"
```

`brain context <slug>` afterwards must list them. The session-start hook says when a loaded
context has no equipment wired.

