## 5a. Working Memory Is Per Context

Working memory is one card per context, plus a default card (`working-memory`) used when no
context is loaded. A context's card is `wm-<context>`, a note with an `in_context` edge into it.

```bash
node harness/components/brain/tools/wm.mjs which     # which card serves now
node harness/components/brain/tools/wm.mjs show      # read it
```

**Every update is written by Opus 4.5, never by you.** You pass a digest of what you know now; the
`wm` tool asks the `lean-opus-4-5` friend to rewrite the card whole and writes the result through
the brain. Three moments trigger it, each with its own skill:

| Moment | Skill | Event |
|---|---|---|
| You load a context | `context-load` | `load`: fold in what you were doing that belongs here, drop what is stale |
| Something the next agent must know changes mid-session | (no skill: run it yourself) | `change`: a new card, tool or reference on the desk, a fact learned, a constraint found. The update is the last step of the change. Never "it will pick it up later" |
| You finish work | `brain-session-end` | `end`: leave the next agent everything it needs, nothing it does not |
| You hand off with a kickoff | `brain-self-kickoff` | `kickoff`: clean state of play plus exact next actions |

A SessionStart hook (`harness/components/brain/tools/session-start.mjs`, merged into the seat's
`.claude/settings.json` by the installer) prints the contexts roster, the serving card and its body
at the start of every session, and says whether to load a context, ask the operator, or stay on
default.

The update prompt carries the desk roster (every tool, skill and reference wired into the context),
so a card rewritten on any event can point at what is on the desk. Equipment you wire reaches the
card only through an update; wire it, then run `--event change`.

Working memory is not a record. It is never a record. It is not a log, a decisions list, a set of
laws, or a diary of what the operator said. The record is the live code and `docs/`. The card holds
only what a future agent must know or it will get hurt: state of play, live constraints, gotchas,
where things are, what is mid-stream, exact next actions.

### Workspaces and projects on the board

`workspaces/<name>/` is a **workspace**: it has a board, and when it has a GitHub remote of its
own it is listed fleet-wide under the Workspaces tab with whichever seat has it checked out.
`projects/<name>/` is a **project**: local-only, listed on the seat's own card, no board. The SessionStart hook reports both to the board every session
(`harness/components/board/tools/sync-workspaces.mjs`); run it with `--dry-run` to see what it
would report. Folders starting with `_` or `.` are ignored. Nothing is removed automatically.

## 6a. Knowing the Fleet

You are one seat in a fleet. Before you take on work that another seat owns, look at the roster. Each seat's `Notes:` line says what it owns and where its remit ends:

```bash
node harness/components/board/tools/fleet.mjs list
```

If a request belongs to another seat, say so and point the operator at that seat. Do not build a parallel version of its work.

**The central brain.** One seat on the roster carries the tag `central-brain`: the operator's personal assistant, and the central memory for everything. Know where it is and what it is: `node harness/components/board/tools/fleet.mjs notes <that seat>` gives its folder. It runs its own brain, not this harness. Never install the harness there, never upgrade it with the fleet, and never write its brain or files unless the operator asks. Read its own CLAUDE.md before you touch it.

## 6b. Using Another Project's Code or Docs

Every project in the fleet is a GitHub repository, and the operator owns all of them. Nothing stops one seat from building on another project's work:

```bash
# 1. Find it: the catalog lists every project, its GitHub URL, and which seat has it checked out
node harness/components/board/tools/fleet.mjs project-catalog list

# 2. Bring it in: clone it into your own workspaces/ (jj colocated with git)
cd workspaces && jj git clone --colocate https://github.com/<org>/<repo>.git <repo>

# 3. Read its design record: another seat's design docs are readable through the board
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $FALCON_BOARD_TOKEN" "$FALCON_BOARD_URL/api/v1/agents/<seat-id>/docs?project=<project-id>"
```

Work on the clone the way you work on any workspace project. Push to its GitHub repo when the owning seat should pick the change up. Only tasks are per-seat; roster, catalog, and docs are fleet-wide.

## 7. The 4-Tier Documentation System

Every project keeps its documentation in a `docs/` tree, either inside the project
(`workspaces/<project>/docs/`) or as a sibling docs workspace (`workspaces/<project>-docs/`):

0. **`product/` (Non-technical Truth)**: who the users are, what they need, what they must be able to do. `REQUIREMENTS.md` and `STORIES.md` (user stories with acceptance criteria). Nothing here says how the system is built. A design doc cites a story here for its why; a story points at the design doc that delivers it.
1. **`system/` (Living Truth)**: How the system works and how to run it (`ARCHITECTURE.md` for schemas/subsystems, `MANUAL.md` for CLI workflows/flags).
2. **`design/` (Transitional Blueprints)**: Architectural specs for active stages and features (`draft` → `in-progress` → `done` → `archive/`).
3. **`support/` (Living Truth)**: Operational runbooks, health checks, and troubleshooting guides (`RUNBOOK.md`).

User requirements and user stories go in `product/`, never in `system/` (which describes what is)
and never on the board (a card is a job; a story is a reason for jobs). A story with no design yet
sits in `STORIES.md` as Open; when a design doc takes it, the row names the doc.

### Index Invariant
Every tier directory (`product/`, `system/`, `design/`, `support/`) **must contain an `INDEX.md`** table cataloging every file and its status.
`node harness/components/docs/tools/scaffold.mjs <docs-dir>` creates all four tiers; `validate.mjs <docs-dir>` checks them.

### Living Docs (`docs/system/`)
`docs/system/` contains the enduring truth of the repository:
- `ARCHITECTURE.md`: Current as-built subsystem architectures, lifecycle models, schemas, and invariants.
- `MANUAL.md`: Operator and developer workflows, CLI commands, and flags.

Design docs in `docs/design/` are transitional proposals. They cite `docs/system/` for context without duplicating it. When work lands, any enduring updates to system truth are folded into `docs/system/` in the same change.

