## 10. Bug Sweeper (`components/sweeper`)

The **Bug Sweeper** is Incubator v5's autonomous quality & invariant assurance engine. It supports both high-throughput Gemini-powered sweeps and deep multi-model audits.

### 10.1 Standard 3-Wave Gemini Sweep
One **sweep** is three **waves** and a judge. Each wave reads the output of the wave before it.

1. **Wave 1 (Breadth & Story Walkthrough)**: High-throughput mental walkthrough matching specs to code via `gemini-3.6-flash`.
2. **Wave 2 (Depth & Operational Reality)**: Invariant auditing, state machine race conditions, and silent data corruption via `gemini-3.8-flash`.
3. **Wave 3 (Dynamic Adversarial Tests)**: Synthesizes and executes real Node.js test scripts (`node:test`) to dynamically verify bugs.
4. **Judge / Gavel**: Adjudicates all candidate findings against dynamic execution proofs, discards speculative trivia, and stamps verified bugs via `gemini-3.8-flash`.

```bash
# Run standard 3-wave sweep over target component
node harness/components/sweeper/tools/sweep.mjs --target workspaces/incubator-v5/components/board --spec workspaces/incubator-v5/docs/design/board.md
```

### 10.2 Multi-Model Deep Sweeper (`--deep`)
When the **`--deep`** flag is passed, the sweeper escalates into a multi-model heterogeneous audit pipeline:
1. **Gemini Fast Baseline**: Generates scope, story context, and initial operational reality scans.
2. **Claude Fable 5.1 (`claude-fable-5-1`)**: Analyzes real-world workflow consistency, edge cases, and ergonomic failure scenarios.
3. **Claude Opus 5.5 (`claude-opus-5-5`, `xhigh` effort)**: The same brief as Fable at the highest reasoning effort, for the faults a faster pass misses. It can take up to 30 minutes.
4. **Codex Astra High (`gpt-6-astra`)**: Deep reasoning sweep focused strictly on security holes, tenant boundary breaches, data loss, and storage invariant violations.
5. **Sane Judge**: Cross-model synthesis, dynamic test execution, and final unified verdict in `.runs/<sweep-id>/deep-summary.md`.

```bash
# Run deep multi-model sweep (Gemini + Fable 5.1 + Opus 5.5 xhigh + Astra High)
node harness/components/sweeper/tools/sweep.mjs --target workspaces/incubator-v5/components/board --spec workspaces/incubator-v5/docs/design/board.md --deep

# Optional controls
node harness/components/sweeper/tools/sweep.mjs --target <target> --deep --effort high --security
```

### 10.3 Sweeps: run one after another, pass the findings back
Words: a **wave** is one model pass. A **sweep** is one full run: three waves and the judge, or the full `--deep` pipeline. "Run another sweep" means a full run on the same target, with the ledger.

One sweep finds the surface bugs. The second and later sweeps find the deep ones, because they know what is fixed, what is rejected and why, and where to look next. That knowledge is the **sweep ledger**. `--prior <file>` gives it to every wave, to the judge, and to every `--deep` model. Standard and deep sweeps read and feed the same ledger.

Each target has one ledger and one folder: `.runs/sweeps/<target-slug>/ledger.md`, with the runs next to it (`sweep-1`, `sweep-2`, `deep-3`, in order).

The loop, for each sweep:
1. **Run.** Pass `--prior` from the second sweep on.
2. **Give a verdict on each stamped bug.** Check it against the real system, not against the report. A generated test that fails is proof of the test, not of the bug: read it.
3. **Fix the real ones** at the root cause. Leave a regression test that fails on the old code.
4. **Write the ledger** (below). Do this before the next sweep, in the same turn as the fix.
5. **Sweep again** when the operator asks, or when a fix changed much code. Stop when a sweep stamps nothing that is real.

The ledger has four parts. Keep it current: change an entry when its state changes. It describes the target now, it is not a diary.
- **Facts**: what you measured about the real system (payload shapes, header formats, limits). These stop false findings; a model that guesses an API shape is the most common false positive.
- **Fixed**: finding, location, what the fix does. Later sweeps check that the fix is complete and look for the same fault in sibling code.
- **Rejected**: finding and the reason. Include what the judge discarded, when you agree. A sweep may raise a rejected finding again only with new proof against the reason.
- **Look next**: areas no sweep has covered, and the code that the last fixes changed most.

```bash
T=.runs/sweeps/board
# sweep 1
node harness/components/sweeper/tools/sweep.mjs --target components/board --spec docs/design/board.md --run-dir $T/sweep-1
# verdicts, fixes, write $T/ledger.md, then sweep 2, 3, ...
node harness/components/sweeper/tools/sweep.mjs --target components/board --spec docs/design/board.md --prior $T/ledger.md --run-dir $T/sweep-2
# a deep sweep takes the same ledger and adds to it
node harness/components/sweeper/tools/sweep.mjs --target components/board --spec docs/design/board.md --prior $T/ledger.md --run-dir $T/deep-3 --deep
```

A good order for code that must not fail: standard sweeps until they go quiet, then one deep sweep with the ledger, then a standard sweep over the deep sweep's fixes.

**Sandbox the run.** Wave 3 runs generated tests with the environment of the sweep command. When the target writes outside the repo (a store in `$HOME`, the macOS Keychain, a live service), start the sweep with the target's paths set to scratch folders, its URLs set to a dead port, and a no-op stand-in first in `PATH` for any system command it calls.

